  "body": "Split from #1616. PR #1632 fixes grblHAL, TinyG and Carvera; this issue is the rest.\n\n**What happens.** fanuc.cps, haas.cps, mazak.cps, doosan-dnm.cps, sinumerik.cps and heidenhain.cps write the stream's first rapid after the tool change and `G43 H..` as one combined move: `G00 X20 Y10 Z-3`. That is a straight line from wherever the tool change left the spindle. It is safe only while that start height is above the part. With a tool too long for the work offset, the line starts below the part top and crosses it. This is the hazard of #1614, which a PathPilot Hub run found on Tormach.\n\n**Expected.** Before its first X/Y traverse after each tool change (and at program start), each post states the section's clearance Z as a Z-only rapid: `ClearancePlane`, else `RetractPlane`, never below the move's own Z. A stream that states neither plane is refused by name. Use the wording of #1618 (Tormach) and #1632 (grblHAL, TinyG, Carvera) so all the posts read alike.\n\n**Care points.**\n- These posts carry G43.4 (TCP) and rotary paths. The Z-only rapid must be in the right frame for each of them, or refused there by name.\n- About 100 goldens and two dozen test files read these posts. Every moved golden must be checked by diff, and only the expected one-line insertion may move.\n- Widen the `assets/posts/AGENTS.md` bullet that #1618 adds to name every post.\n\n**Done means:** each of the six posts writes the clearance Z before its first X/Y traverse after a tool change; a stream with no stated plane is refused by name; pinned per post with mutation checks; moved goldens listed in the PR.\n",
